#!/usr/bin/env bash
# claudemanager — the CLAUDEMANAGER warm session, one command.
# The manager's brain runs inside tmux session "cs-manager" (claudestart family:
# `claudestart list` sees it, phone picks it up via claude.ai/code as "MANAGER").
#   claudemanager           start it (safe to run twice — won't duplicate)
#   claudemanager stop      stop it + disable watchdog respawn (until next start)
#   claudemanager rotate    clean recycle NOW: handover marker → kill → fresh session
#   claudemanager solo      Marc is running one BY HAND: stop the tmux one, no respawn
#                           until the 2am rotate (prevents two brains writing the board)
#   claudemanager console   watch it live (detach: Ctrl+B then D)
#   claudemanager status    one-line health check
#   claudemanager unstick   clear a dialog that is blocking the message queue
#   claudemanager handover-check [--consume]
#                           is the handover marker a real relevo, or a crash wearing its clothes?
#   claudemanager watchdog  (for cron) clear a stuck dialog · respawn if DEAD · recycle only a CORPSE
#                           · log the memory curve · warn at 6 GB · graceful rotate at 16 GB / <2 GB free
#
# WHEN THE SESSION IS REPLACED (Marc's rule, 12 Aug 2026 — "me molesta que el agente
# decida cerrar y abrir sesión… prefiero mantener un agente más tiempo para conservar
# contexto"):
#   • DAILY at 2:00am  → cron runs `claudemanager rotate` (clean, silent handover)
#   • ON HIS WORD      → `claudemanager rotate`, or the agent recycling when he asks
#   • PROVEN BLOCKED  → the liveness ladder replaces it (rung 3, Marc approved 15 Aug
#     2026). Not "quiet": blocked — heartbeat past 90 min AND the screen frozen AND no
#     turn running, and two Escapes already failed. A stopped session has no context
#     worth keeping; everything real is on disk.
#   • NEVER on a stale heartbeat alone. A quiet session is not a broken session, and
#     killing it throws away the context that makes him fast. The old rule (recycle any
#     live session whose heartbeat was >2h old) is GONE.
# The watchdog still respawns a session that is genuinely DEAD, still clears a blocking
# dialog, and still recycles a CORPSE — but a corpse now has to prove it is one: the
# usage-limit banner on screen AND a stale heartbeat (the 10 Aug 2026 signature, where
# every respawn booted into "You've reached your Fable 5 limit" and made zero tool calls).
#
# THE MEMORY GUARD (18 Aug 2026, Marc's design after the box OOM-killed the manager at
# 21:30:55 — kernel: "Out of memory: Killed process … anon-rss 11.3 GB"). A manager that
# works a full day grows: 0.6 GB fresh, ~1.1 GB/hour under load, 11.3 GB at 9.5 hours.
# Nothing watched that number, so the kernel made the decision instead — no warning, no
# brief written, and the fresh session mistook the crash for a clean relevo.
#   · every run  → the memory curve, one CSV line, claude and children counted apart
#                  (Marc's ask: it names WHICH half leaks — session heap or MCP servers)
#   · 6 GB       → ONE warning down Marc's own `mgr` line. The live manager reads it in
#                  seconds and brings it to him with a recommendation. He decides.
#   · 16 GB, or under 2 GB available box-wide while the manager itself is over 4 GB
#                → GRACEFUL rotate: order the manager to write its brief (/manager-close),
#                  give it 15 minutes, then replace it. Wrote the brief → marker set, the
#                  successor takes over silently. Too frozen to write → killed anyway, NO
#                  marker, so the successor confesses and recovers from the transcript.
#   · always     → the manager is the kernel's DESIGNATED VICTIM (oom_score_adj on its
#                  whole process tree). A sudden spike no watchdog can outrun shoots the
#                  manager, never postgres or a gunicorn worker. The manager's death is
#                  survivable by design; production's is not.
# The 4 GB floor on the low-memory trigger is deliberate: when the box fills up and the
# manager is NOT the cause, replacing it frees nothing and burns the context Marc's
# 12 Aug rule exists to protect.

# SESSION and the state-file prefix are overridable for ONE reason: the watchdog's
# kill path (rung 3, below) must be truth-table tested against a throwaway tmux
# session instead of Marc's live manager. Unset in production — the defaults are
# byte-identical to the values used before this was added (15 Aug 2026).
SESSION="${CLAUDEMANAGER_SESSION:-cs-manager}"
STATE="${CLAUDEMANAGER_STATE:-$HOME/.claudemanager}"
TITLE="MANAGER"
WORKDIR="$HOME"   # home, not NORTH_STAR: keeps personas/, skills/, NORTH_STAR/ all in-scope so auto-mode never stalls on permission prompts
HEART="$STATE.heartbeat"
OFF="$STATE.off"
HANDOVER="$STATE.handover"
# SOLO: "a hand-started manager is the live brain right now — watchdog, keep your hands
# off." Marc runs managers by hand often (a bare `claude` in iTerm2); when he does, the
# 5-minute watchdog would spawn a SECOND brain inside tmux and both would write the
# board — two hands on one wheel. Different from OFF: `stop` means stopped until Marc
# starts it again, while SOLO is cleared by the 2am rotate, which is exactly what he
# wants — his hand-started session tonight, a clean managed one from 2am on.
SOLO="$STATE.solo"
PANE_FP="$STATE.pane"    # previous watchdog run's screen fingerprint — see the ladder below
STUCK="$STATE.stuck"     # "<epoch first seen blocked> <escape attempts made>"
PARKSEEN="$STATE.parkseen"  # epoch of the FIRST parked sighting — Escape only on the second.
                            # 24 Aug 2026: a single-glance parked() false-positive Escaped a
                            # HEALTHY booting session mid-tool-call ("Request interrupted by
                            # user for tool use", 00:00:01) and doomed it. A real orphaned
                            # modal is still there 5 minutes later; a false positive never is.
# rotate refuses to replace a session younger than this (24 Aug 2026: the 02:00 cron
# killed a session born 01:45 — 15 minutes of context for zero benefit, and every
# succession strands Marc's open phone chat). Overridable for the truth-table test only.
ROTATE_MIN_AGE="${CLAUDEMANAGER_ROTATE_MIN_AGE:-1800}"
STALE_SECS=7200   # two missed hourly beats = dead brain
# BLOCKED_SECS — the liveness ladder's threshold (15 Aug 2026). A WORKING manager
# touches its heartbeat every hop, hourly. 90 minutes is 1.5 missed beats: past any
# healthy gap, yet early enough that a 2am freeze is repaired hours before the 5:10
# brief. Deliberately ABOVE the 60-minute hop interval — a shorter value would flag a
# perfectly healthy manager that is simply sitting between wakes, which is exactly the
# session Marc's 12 Aug rule protects.
BLOCKED_SECS=5400
MODEL_PRIMARY="claude-fable-5[1m]"    # Marc's requirement (3 Aug 2026): Fable + max effort
MODEL_FALLBACK="claude-opus-5[1m]"    # if Fable is unavailable (usage limits)

# ── MEMORY GUARD state + thresholds (18 Aug 2026) ────────────────────────────
MEMLOG="$STATE.mem"          # the curve: one CSV line per watchdog run, ~288/day
MEMWARN="$STATE.memwarn"     # tmux session-created stamp already warned about (one warning per session)
EMERG="$STATE.emergency"     # "<epoch ordered> <session stamp>" — a graceful rotate is in flight
# Marc's direct line: the manager tails it, so it is also how the watchdog TELLS the
# manager something. Derived from $STATE on purpose — the production path is unchanged
# ($HOME/.claudemanager.msgs), and the truth-table test redirects it with everything else
# instead of writing into Marc's real inbox.
MSGS="$STATE.msgs"
WARN_GB=6                    # 10× a fresh session (0.6 GB measured) — loud enough to mean something, quiet enough never to nag
CEIL_GB=16                   # Marc's number, 18 Aug 2026 ("16, not 12")
FREE_MIN_GB=2                # box-wide MemAvailable floor — tonight's real condition
MEM_FLOOR_GB=4               # …but only rotate for it if the manager is actually part of the problem
EMERG_GRACE=900              # 15 minutes to write the brief, then the emergency wins
OOM_ADJ=500                  # designated victim: shoot the manager first, never prod
# A handover marker states WHY it exists (18 Aug 2026). A marker with a sanctioned
# reason is a real relevo at any age — solo sets one at 14:00 that the 2am rotate
# consumes 12 hours later. A BARE marker (a plain `touch`, the old habit) is only
# trusted while it is fresh: the 21:30 crash was disguised as a clean takeover by a
# marker touched 9.5 hours earlier "as a safety net".
MARKER_FRESH_SECS=10800      # 3h — a bare marker older than this is a crash in disguise
# The current board's close marker. The graceful rotate waits for THIS file's mtime to
# move: the close ritual's own Step 7 writes it, so it is the one unambiguous "the brief
# is written" signal. Swap the path when the board changes (persona § Current Board).
CLOSE_SHA="${CLAUDEMANAGER_CLOSE_SHA:-$HOME/NORTH_STAR/BOARDS/PROJECT_HERO/.last-close-sha}"

# Is a turn RUNNING right now? Defined up here (moved 15 Aug 2026) because the watchdog
# now needs it too: sending Escape into a running turn CANCELS that turn, so every
# key the watchdog sends is gated on this being false.
# The discriminator is "<verb>… (<timer>" — an ellipsis plus a live timer:
#   running → "✻ Orchestrating… (4s · thinking with max effort)"
#   running → "· Photosynthesizing… (29s · ↓ 625 tokens)"
#   running → "✽ Precipitating… (2m 20s · ↓ 6.9k tokens · thinking with max effort)"
#   idle    → "✻ Worked for 17s"   (past tense, stays on screen after the turn ends)
# Do NOT anchor on the glyph: it animates through ✻, ·, ✽, so a glyph-based match reads
# a busy session as idle — the bug class that caused the 4 Aug freeze.
# ⚠️ FIXED 15 Aug 2026: the old pattern ended `\([0-9]+s` and therefore went BLIND the
# moment a turn passed 60 seconds, because the timer switches from "(45s" to "(2m 20s".
# Measured live: the manager was mid-turn on "Precipitating… (2m 20s" and the old regex
# matched zero. Trailing `[ms]` covers both forms. `\([0-9]` also keeps it from matching
# "Reading 1 file… (ctrl+o to expand)", which is not a timer.
BUSY_RE="[A-Za-z]+… \([0-9]+[ms]"

alive() { tmux has-session -t "$SESSION" 2>/dev/null; }
busy()  { tmux capture-pane -t "$SESSION" -p 2>/dev/null | grep -qE "$BUSY_RE"; }

# The screen, as one hash. A session that is doing ANYTHING repaints; a session frozen
# behind a modal is byte-identical run after run. This is content-free on purpose — it
# knows nothing about which dialog is up, so a Claude Code release that ships a brand
# new dialog cannot blind it (autoUpdatesChannel is "latest": four versions in three
# days). It is the redundant half of the ladder; the heartbeat is the primary signal.
pane_fingerprint() { tmux capture-pane -t "$SESSION" -p 2>/dev/null | md5sum | cut -d' ' -f1; }

# A modal dialog left unanswered blocks the ENTIRE message queue: Marc's messages
# enqueue and are never read, and scheduled wake-ups never fire — while the session
# still looks alive. This is what froze the manager on 4 Aug 2026 for 22 minutes.
parked() { tmux capture-pane -t "$SESSION" -p 2>/dev/null | grep -qE "❯ 1\.|Switch model\?"; }

# A CORPSE: alive as a process, brain-dead as an agent. The 10 Aug 2026 signature —
# the session boots straight into the usage-limit banner, makes zero tool calls, and
# can therefore never touch its own heartbeat. Requires BOTH signals: the banner on
# screen AND a stale heartbeat. Never the heartbeat alone — that would kill the quiet
# healthy sessions Marc wants kept.
limited() { tmux capture-pane -t "$SESSION" -p 2>/dev/null | grep -qiE "reached your .*limit|/usage-credits|usage limit"; }

# A BOOT-CORPSE of the transient kind (24 Aug 2026): session d7493b80 booted into
# "API Error: 529 Overloaded" (server-side), made zero tool calls, and sat 94 minutes
# until rung 3 killed it. A 5xx heals in minutes — replace fast and let the next
# watchdog run respawn. Detection needs THREE signals (see the branch below): the
# error class on screen, a stale heartbeat, AND a heartbeat that has never beaten
# since the session was born — so a WORKING manager whose pane merely QUOTES an API
# error (forensics output, pasted logs) can never match: its heartbeat has moved.
overloaded() { tmux capture-pane -t "$SESSION" -p 2>/dev/null | grep -qE "API Error: 5[0-9][0-9]"; }

heartbeat_age() {
  [ -f "$HEART" ] || { echo 999999; return; }
  echo $(( $(date +%s) - $(stat -c %Y "$HEART") ))
}

# ── MEMORY GUARD helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The pane's pid, and every process under it. Why the whole tree and not one pid:
# the 21:30 OOM victim was pid 1213133 with comm "2.1.234" — a version-named process
# that was NOT the pid tmux started (997450). Claude Code re-spawns itself on in-place
# updates (autoUpdatesChannel: latest), so anything that must find "the manager" walks
# the tree. The MCP servers live in the same tree and are counted separately below.
# ⚠️ `if (seen[p]==1)` on the way out is load-bearing, not style. awk CREATES an array
# entry the moment you read one, so `seen[par[p]]` inside the walk quietly adds every
# parent pid on the box — and the first version printed them all. Caught by the truth
# table on 18 Aug 2026 before it ever ran from cron, but not before the test run itself
# had flagged ~156 processes (app_cc gunicorn workers among them) as OOM victims; all
# were reset the same minute. A tree walk that can return the whole process table is a
# loaded gun pointed at production.
mgr_tree() {
  local root="$1"; [ -n "$root" ] || return 0
  ps -eo pid=,ppid= 2>/dev/null | awk -v root="$root" '
    { pid[NR]=$1; par[$1]=$2; n=NR }
    END {
      seen[root]=1; changed=1
      while (changed) { changed=0
        for (i=1;i<=n;i++) { p=pid[i]; if (seen[p]!=1 && seen[par[p]]==1) { seen[p]=1; changed=1 } } }
      for (p in seen) if (seen[p]==1) print p
    }'
}

# "<pane_kb> <children_kb> <available_kb>". MemAvailable, not MemFree: cache is
# reclaimable, and it is what the kernel actually has left before it starts shooting.
# The FAKE_* overrides are TEST-ONLY (same precedent as CLAUDEMANAGER_NO_START): every
# threshold below must be provable without putting real memory pressure on a box that
# has already OOM-killed once tonight.
mem_snapshot() {
  local pane="$1" p kb pane_kb=0 child_kb=0 avail_kb
  if [ -n "${CLAUDEMANAGER_FAKE_RSS_KB:-}" ]; then
    pane_kb="$CLAUDEMANAGER_FAKE_RSS_KB"; child_kb="${CLAUDEMANAGER_FAKE_CHILD_KB:-0}"
  else
    for p in $(mgr_tree "$pane"); do
      kb=$(awk '/^VmRSS:/{print $2}' "/proc/$p/status" 2>/dev/null) || continue
      [ -n "$kb" ] || continue
      if [ "$p" = "$pane" ]; then pane_kb=$((pane_kb + kb)); else child_kb=$((child_kb + kb)); fi
    done
  fi
  avail_kb="${CLAUDEMANAGER_FAKE_AVAIL_KB:-$(awk '/^MemAvailable:/{print $2}' /proc/meminfo)}"
  echo "$pane_kb $child_kb ${avail_kb:-0}"
}

# Tell the kernel who to shoot when the box runs out in a single breath — faster than
# any 5-minute check can react. Raising oom_score_adj needs no privileges; the whole
# manager tree gets it, so an in-place update cannot quietly drop the protection.
# The blast radius is capped on purpose (18 Aug 2026). A manager tree is a handful of
# processes — claude plus its MCP servers. If the walk ever returns a crowd, the walk is
# broken, and marking the box's processes as preferred OOM victims is the exact opposite
# of this feature's job. Refuse and log instead of acting on a bad answer.
OOM_TREE_MAX=25
oom_protect() {
  local pane="$1" p tree count
  tree=$(mgr_tree "$pane"); count=$(printf '%s\n' "$tree" | grep -c .)
  if [ "$count" -gt "$OOM_TREE_MAX" ]; then
    echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] watchdog: REFUSING oom_score_adj — tree walk returned $count pids for pane $pane (expected ≤$OOM_TREE_MAX); not touching anything"
    return 0
  fi
  for p in $tree; do
    echo "$OOM_ADJ" > "/proc/$p/oom_score_adj" 2>/dev/null || true
  done
}

# Is the handover marker a real relevo, or a crash wearing its clothes?
# Prints: "CLEAN <reason> <age>s" · "STALE bare-marker <age>s" · "NONE"
marker_verdict() {
  [ -f "$HANDOVER" ] || { echo "NONE"; return; }
  local age reason
  age=$(( $(date +%s) - $(stat -c %Y "$HANDOVER") ))
  reason=$(awk 'NR==1{print $1}' "$HANDOVER" 2>/dev/null)
  case "$reason" in
    rotate|solo|brief|emergency-rotate|blocked-rung3|manual)
      echo "CLEAN $reason ${age}s" ;;
    *)
      if [ "$age" -lt "$MARKER_FRESH_SECS" ]; then echo "CLEAN bare-marker ${age}s"
      else echo "STALE bare-marker ${age}s"; fi ;;
  esac
}

# Every writer of the marker states its reason, so age never has to be guessed at.
set_marker() { printf '%s %s\n' "$1" "$(date +%s)" > "$HANDOVER"; }

case "$1" in
  console)
    exec tmux attach -t "$SESSION"
    ;;
  status)
    if alive; then S="ALIVE"; else S="DEAD"; fi
    AGE=$(heartbeat_age)
    [ -f "$OFF" ] && O=" · OFF-SWITCH SET (watchdog will not respawn)" || O=""
    [ -f "$HANDOVER" ] && H=" · handover marker present" || H=""
    [ -f "$SOLO" ] && O="$O · 🙋 SOLO MODE (hand-started manager owns it until the 2am rotate)"
    P=""; alive && parked && P=" · ⚠️  PARKED ON A DIALOG — queue blocked, run: claudemanager unstick"
    echo "manager: $S · heartbeat ${AGE}s ago${O}${H}${P}"
    exit 0
    ;;
  handover-check)
    # The boot's first question: was I relieved, or did I die? `mgr-opencheck` calls this
    # with --consume at every boot, so the answer is MECHANICAL — the persona used to ask
    # the agent to check the marker by hand, and on 18 Aug that hand-check accepted a
    # 9.5-hour-old bare marker and reported a clean takeover over a kernel OOM kill.
    # One definition of "is this marker real", used by the boot and by the watchdog.
    marker_verdict
    [ "$2" = "--consume" ] && rm -f "$HANDOVER"
    exit 0
    ;;
  unstick)
    # Answer whatever dialog the session is parked on, so the queue drains and any
    # messages typed while it was stuck get read. Safe to run when nothing is stuck.
    if ! alive; then echo "Manager is not running."; exit 0; fi
    if parked; then
      tmux send-keys -t "$SESSION" Enter
      sleep 3
      if parked; then
        echo "⚠️  Still parked. Answer it by hand: claudemanager console"
      else
        echo "✅ Dialog cleared — queued messages are flushing now."
      fi
    else
      echo "✅ Nothing parked; queue is flowing."
    fi
    exit 0
    ;;
  stop)
    touch "$OFF"
    if alive; then
      tmux kill-session -t "$SESSION"
      echo "🛑 Manager stopped. Watchdog respawn disabled until: claudemanager"
    else
      echo "Nothing was running. Watchdog respawn disabled until: claudemanager"
    fi
    exit 0
    ;;
  solo)
    # "I am running the manager by hand — stand down until the 2am rotate."
    touch "$SOLO"
    set_marker solo   # so the 2am session takes over silently, no death to confess.
                      # Reason-stamped: a solo marker is legitimately hours old by the
                      # time the rotate consumes it, and age alone must never call it stale.
    if alive; then
      tmux kill-session -t "$SESSION"
      echo "🙋 Solo mode: tmux manager stopped. The watchdog will NOT respawn it."
    else
      echo "🙋 Solo mode set. The watchdog will NOT spawn a manager."
    fi
    echo "   Your hand-started session is the brain until 02:00, when rotate takes over."
    exit 0
    ;;
  rotate|renew)
    # The ONLY sanctioned way a healthy session gets replaced: on the 2am cron, or
    # because Marc asked. Sets the handover marker first, so the fresh session takes
    # over silently instead of confessing a death that never happened.
    if [ -f "$OFF" ]; then
      echo "🛑 Off-switch is set — not rotating. Start it with: claudemanager"
      exit 0
    fi
    rm -f "$SOLO"   # solo mode ends here: from now on the script owns the brain again
    rm -f "$EMERG"  # a scheduled rotate satisfies any emergency rotate in flight
    if alive; then
      # Born minutes ago? Replacing it buys nothing and costs Marc's phone binding —
      # every succession strands whatever chat view he has open (24 Aug 2026: the
      # 02:00 cron killed a session born 01:45, 15 minutes old).
      BORN=$(tmux display -p -t "$SESSION" '#{session_created}' 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
      case "$BORN" in ''|*[!0-9]*) BORN=0 ;; esac
      if [ "$BORN" -gt 0 ] && [ $(( $(date +%s) - BORN )) -lt "$ROTATE_MIN_AGE" ]; then
        echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] rotate: session is only $(( ( $(date +%s) - BORN ) / 60 )) min old — already fresh, SKIPPING replacement"
        exit 0
      fi
      set_marker rotate
      echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] rotate: clean recycle (handover marker set)"
      tmux kill-session -t "$SESSION"
      sleep 2
    else
      echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] rotate: nothing was running — starting fresh"
    fi
    ;;
  watchdog)
    [ -f "$OFF" ] && exit 0
    [ -f "$SOLO" ] && exit 0   # Marc is running a manager by hand — never duplicate it
    if alive; then
      FP=$(pane_fingerprint); PREV=$(cat "$PANE_FP" 2>/dev/null || echo "")
      printf '%s\n' "$FP" > "$PANE_FP"
      AGE=$(heartbeat_age)

      # ── THE MEMORY GUARD (18 Aug 2026) ──────────────────────────────────────────
      # Runs before everything else: a box with no memory left is a harder deadline
      # than a frozen screen, and unlike the ladder it protects production too.
      PANE_PID=$(tmux list-panes -t "$SESSION" -F '#{pane_pid}' 2>/dev/null | head -1)
      SESS_STAMP=$(tmux display -p -t "$SESSION" '#{session_created}' 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
      if [ -n "$PANE_PID" ]; then
        oom_protect "$PANE_PID"
        read -r MEM_KB CHILD_KB AVAIL_KB <<< "$(mem_snapshot "$PANE_PID")"
        TREE_KB=$((MEM_KB + CHILD_KB))
        # The curve, in a file of its own so the watchdog log keeps only signal. Every
        # line is claude and children apart: a leak that grows while the manager sits
        # idle is a streaming/MCP buffer, one that jumps per hop is per-turn retention.
        printf '%s,%s,%s,%s,%s\n' "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')" "$MEM_KB" "$CHILD_KB" "$AVAIL_KB" "$AGE" >> "$MEMLOG"
        if [ "$(wc -l < "$MEMLOG" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)" -gt 4000 ]; then
          tail -3000 "$MEMLOG" > "$MEMLOG.trim" && mv "$MEMLOG.trim" "$MEMLOG"
        fi
        MEM_GB=$((TREE_KB / 1048576)); AVAIL_GB=$((AVAIL_KB / 1048576))

        # 6 GB — ONE warning per session, down Marc's own line, for the MANAGER to
        # carry to him with a recommendation. The watchdog never decides here: Marc's
        # 12 Aug rule says a session is replaced on the 2am cron or on his word.
        if [ "$MEM_GB" -ge "$WARN_GB" ] && [ "$(cat "$MEMWARN" 2>/dev/null)" != "$SESS_STAMP" ]; then
          printf '%s\n' "$SESS_STAMP" > "$MEMWARN"
          echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] watchdog: MEMORY WARNING — manager tree ${MEM_GB}GB (claude $((MEM_KB/1048576))GB + children $((CHILD_KB/1048576))GB) · box available ${AVAIL_GB}GB — warned Marc's line once"
          printf '[%s] WATCHDOG (memory guard): the manager session is at %sGB (fresh is ~0.6GB) · box available %sGB. Ceiling %sGB = automatic graceful rotate. Flush anything unwritten to the board now, and bring Marc the option of an early rotate with a recommendation.\n' \
            "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %Z')" "$MEM_GB" "$AVAIL_GB" "$CEIL_GB" >> "$MSGS"
        fi

        # The ceiling, and tonight's real condition (a full box with the manager in it).
        # The 4 GB floor keeps a small manager from being churned for someone else's leak.
        if [ "$MEM_GB" -ge "$CEIL_GB" ] || { [ "$AVAIL_GB" -lt "$FREE_MIN_GB" ] && [ "$MEM_GB" -ge "$MEM_FLOOR_GB" ]; }; then
          if [ ! -f "$EMERG" ]; then
            # Not a kill — an ORDER. The manager is told to write its brief, exactly as
            # an assistant leaving mid-shift briefs the one taking over. Everything that
            # matters ends up on disk BEFORE the session is replaced.
            printf '%s %s\n' "$(date +%s)" "$SESS_STAMP" > "$EMERG"
            echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] watchdog: EMERGENCY ROTATE ORDERED — tree ${MEM_GB}GB · available ${AVAIL_GB}GB — manager has $((EMERG_GRACE/60))min to write its brief"
            printf '[%s] WATCHDOG (memory guard): 🚨 EMERGENCY ROTATE — manager tree %sGB · box available %sGB. Run /manager-close NOW (digest Today block + log rows + close SHA + push). You have %s minutes; then this session is replaced. Write the brief so your successor needs no re-explaining.\n' \
              "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %Z')" "$MEM_GB" "$AVAIL_GB" "$((EMERG_GRACE/60))" >> "$MSGS"
            exit 0
          fi
        fi

        # An emergency already ordered: has the brief landed?
        if [ -f "$EMERG" ]; then
          read -r EM_AT EM_STAMP <<< "$(cat "$EMERG")"
          case "$EM_AT" in ''|*[!0-9]*) EM_AT=0 ;; esac
          if [ "$EM_STAMP" != "$SESS_STAMP" ]; then
            rm -f "$EMERG"   # a different session is running now — the order died with the old one
          else
            CLOSE_AT=$(stat -c %Y "$CLOSE_SHA" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
            if [ "$CLOSE_AT" -ge "$EM_AT" ]; then
              echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] watchdog: emergency rotate — brief WRITTEN (close SHA moved) — clean replacement"
              rm -f "$EMERG" "$MEMWARN"
              set_marker emergency-rotate   # brief on disk → the successor takes over silently
              tmux kill-session -t "$SESSION"
              exit 0
            elif [ $(( $(date +%s) - EM_AT )) -ge "$EMERG_GRACE" ]; then
              # Too frozen (or too busy) to write. The memory pressure is real and
              # production is behind it, so the replacement happens anyway — with NO
              # marker, so the successor confesses the death and recovers the unwritten
              # stretch from the dead session's transcript (mgr-opencheck prints how).
              echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] watchdog: emergency rotate — grace expired with NO close — replacing WITHOUT marker (successor confesses + recovers from transcript)"
              rm -f "$EMERG" "$MEMWARN"
              tmux kill-session -t "$SESSION"
              exit 0
            fi
          fi
        fi
      fi

      # ── FAST PATH · a dialog is on screen ───────────────────────────────────────
      # Clear it WITHOUT killing the session, so nothing Marc typed is lost. Marc
      # switches models mid-conversation often, across devices (his words, 4 Aug 2026);
      # if a confirm ever gets orphaned, this heals it instead of stranding him.
      # ESCAPE, never Enter (changed 15 Aug 2026). Enter selects whatever is highlighted
      # — a decision, made blind, by a cron, on Marc's behalf. That morning it answered
      # option 1 of "Set up auto mode for your environment?". Escape decides nothing.
      # And never send a key into a RUNNING turn: Escape would cancel Marc's own work.
      if parked && ! busy; then
        if [ -f "$PARKSEEN" ]; then
          echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] watchdog: dialog on screen (2nd consecutive sighting) — sending Escape (dismiss, decide nothing)"
          tmux send-keys -t "$SESSION" Escape; sleep 2; tmux send-keys -t "$SESSION" Escape
          # Deliberately NO exit and NO all-clear here. On 15 Aug the blind Enter made the
          # regex stop matching, the watchdog logged success, and the session stayed frozen
          # for seven more hours. "The dialog text went away" is not evidence of recovery.
          # Only the ladder below is allowed to decide whether this manager is working.
        else
          # First sighting: suspect, don't shoot (24 Aug 2026). parked() is a content
          # match ("❯ 1.") that can race a turn transition or match agent-printed text —
          # at 00:00:01 a single-glance Escape interrupted a healthy boot mid-tool-call
          # and doomed the session. A real orphaned modal survives one 5-min cycle; a
          # false positive clears itself. Cost: one extra cycle before a true dialog
          # is dismissed — the 4 Aug class froze for 22+ min, so 5 min is cheap.
          date +%s > "$PARKSEEN"
          echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] watchdog: dialog suspected on screen — waiting one cycle to confirm before any key is sent"
        fi
      elif ! parked; then
        rm -f "$PARKSEEN"   # whatever we saw is gone — it was a false positive or got answered
      fi

      # ── BOOT-CORPSE · transient API overload — replace fast (24 Aug 2026) ────────
      # Three signals, all required: the 5xx error on screen · heartbeat past 15 min ·
      # heartbeat NEVER beaten since this session was born (the start path touches it
      # ~1s before spawn, so a corpse's heartbeat mtime ≈ birth; any session that ever
      # ran its boot has moved it past birth+120). That third signal is what makes a
      # healthy manager that merely PRINTED "API Error: 529" in forensics output
      # unkillable here — its heartbeat beats hourly.
      SESS_BORN="${CLAUDEMANAGER_FAKE_BORN:-$SESS_STAMP}"
      HB_AT=$(stat -c %Y "$HEART" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
      if [ "$AGE" -ge 900 ] && ! busy && [ "$SESS_BORN" -gt 0 ] && [ "$HB_AT" -le $((SESS_BORN + 120)) ] && overloaded; then
        echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] watchdog: BOOT-CORPSE — API 5xx on screen + heartbeat ${AGE}s stale and never beaten since birth — replacing now (fast retry; NO marker, successor confesses)"
        rm -f "$STUCK" "$PANE_FP" "$PARKSEEN"
        tmux kill-session -t "$SESSION"
        exit 0
      fi

      # ── THE LIVENESS LADDER · is this manager producing work? ────────────────────
      # Content-free on purpose: it never asks WHAT is on screen, only whether the
      # manager is doing its job — so a Claude Code release that ships an unknown new
      # dialog cannot blind it. Three conditions, all required:
      #   · heartbeat older than 90 min      — a working manager beats hourly
      #   · screen identical to last run     — a working manager repaints
      #   · no turn running                  — a long task is work, not a freeze
      # A quiet-but-healthy manager fails the first test; a busy one fails the third.
      # Only a genuinely stopped session gets in here.
      # ENTERING the ladder is conservative — it needs the frozen screen as corroboration.
      # STAYING in it deliberately does not: our own Escape repaints the pane, and a
      # repaint we caused is not evidence of work. Once a freeze has been confirmed the
      # heartbeat is the sole authority, and only a fresh beat (or a running turn) clears
      # the state. Found by the truth-table test, 15 Aug 2026: without this the attempt
      # counter reset on every Escape and rung 3 could never be reached — the watchdog
      # would have Escaped a dead session forever.
      if [ "$AGE" -ge "$BLOCKED_SECS" ] && ! busy \
         && { [ -f "$STUCK" ] || { [ -n "$PREV" ] && [ "$FP" = "$PREV" ]; }; }; then
        read -r SINCE ATT <<< "$(cat "$STUCK" 2>/dev/null || echo '0 0')"
        case "$SINCE" in ''|*[!0-9]*) SINCE=0 ;; esac
        case "$ATT"   in ''|*[!0-9]*) ATT=0   ;; esac
        NOW=$(date +%s); [ "$SINCE" -eq 0 ] && SINCE=$NOW
        ATT=$((ATT+1)); printf '%s %s\n' "$SINCE" "$ATT" > "$STUCK"
        if [ "$ATT" -lt 3 ]; then
          echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] watchdog: BLOCKED (heartbeat ${AGE}s · screen frozen · no turn running) — Escape ${ATT}/2"
          tmux send-keys -t "$SESSION" Escape; sleep 2; tmux send-keys -t "$SESSION" Escape
          exit 0
        fi
        # RUNG 3 — approved by Marc, 15 Aug 2026. Two Escapes failed. This session is
        # not thinking, it is stopped, and a stopped session has no context worth
        # keeping — everything real is on disk. The marker makes the respawn a SILENT
        # clean takeover. This does NOT weaken Marc's 12 Aug rule: a quiet, responsive
        # manager can never reach this line. The respawn itself is left to the next
        # watchdog run (≤5 min), which takes the ordinary "manager is down" path —
        # no new start logic, nothing else in this branch chain disturbed.
        echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] watchdog: BLOCKED $((NOW-SINCE))s after 2 Escapes — replacing the session (rung 3)"
        rm -f "$STUCK" "$PANE_FP" "$PARKSEEN"
        set_marker blocked-rung3
        tmux kill-session -t "$SESSION"
        exit 0
      else
        rm -f "$STUCK"   # working again, or never stuck — reset the ladder
      fi
      # A stale heartbeat on its own is NOT a reason to kill anything (Marc, 12 Aug
      # 2026). A session can be quiet for hours and be perfectly alive — and its
      # context is worth more than a tidy wake chain. Only a proven CORPSE is recycled:
      # usage-limit banner on screen AND heartbeat stale.
      if [ "$AGE" -lt "$STALE_SECS" ]; then exit 0; fi
      # A pre-touched handover marker + a stale heartbeat = the manager SANCTIONED its
      # own replacement (the 5:10 brief hop ends chain-dead by design with the marker
      # already set — 12 Aug rule). Rotating here honors the manager's own declared
      # handover; it is NOT the watchdog deciding. Everything is on disk, the respawn
      # takes over silently, zero context burned that the manager didn't write down.
      # Closes the 13 Aug contradiction (manager dark 05:14→17:01: the brief rule
      # trusted a recycle this script no longer performed; agents' messages and the
      # 3pm alarm waited ~12h for a tool round that never came).
      # ⚠️ The marker must be a REAL one (18 Aug 2026). A bare marker touched hours
      # earlier "as a safety net" is not a sanction — under the old test it could have
      # rotated a perfectly healthy quiet session on someone else's leftover file.
      MARKER=$(marker_verdict)
      if [ "${MARKER%% *}" = "CLEAN" ]; then
        echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] watchdog: heartbeat ${AGE}s stale WITH a sanctioned handover marker ($MARKER) — manager sanctioned its replacement; rotating"
        tmux kill-session -t "$SESSION"
        # marker stays in place → the fresh session takes over silently (clean handover)
      elif ! limited; then
        echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] watchdog: heartbeat ${AGE}s stale but session is healthy — LEAVING IT ALONE (rotate runs at 2am)"
        exit 0
      else
        echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] watchdog: CORPSE — usage-limit banner + heartbeat ${AGE}s stale — recycling"
        tmux kill-session -t "$SESSION"
        # no handover marker on purpose: an unclean death must be confessed
      fi
    else
      echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] watchdog: manager is down — respawning"
    fi
    ;;
esac

# ---- start path (default, and watchdog fall-through) ----

# Test-only stop line (15 Aug 2026). Lets the truth-table test exercise every watchdog
# decision without ever spawning a real Claude session. Unset in production.
[ -n "${CLAUDEMANAGER_NO_START:-}" ] && { echo "[test] CLAUDEMANAGER_NO_START set — stopping before the start path"; exit 0; }

exec 9>"$HOME/.claudemanager.lock"
if ! flock -n 9; then
  echo "Another claudemanager start is already in progress."
  exit 0
fi
case "$1" in watchdog|rotate|renew) : ;; *) rm -f "$OFF" ;; esac

if alive; then
  echo "✅ Manager already running as '$TITLE' (tmux: $SESSION)."
  echo "   Watch: claudemanager console · Health: claudemanager status"
  exit 0
fi

touch "$HEART"   # grace period so the watchdog never kill-loops a booting session

# `--name "MANAGER"` (added 12 Aug 2026) is what makes OTHER sessions see this one as
# "MANAGER" in /list-agents and in the session picker. `--remote-control` only names it
# for the phone: measured live, the script-started manager was listed by its peers as
# "ubuntu-b5 · tmux cs-manager" while a separate stale row read "MANAGER · Remote Control
# · offline". Marc could not tell which row to message, and the manager had to grep
# transcripts to find him back. One flag closes both holes.
# Model + effort are set at LAUNCH, never by typing /model or /effort into the TUI.
# Root cause of the 4 Aug 2026 freeze: a slash command typed while a turn is running
# gets QUEUED. It pops later and opens a "Switch model?" modal that nobody is there to
# answer — and an unanswered modal blocks the whole message queue. Marc's next 5
# messages piled up unread and the scheduled wake-up never fired, all while the session
# looked perfectly healthy. Launch flags open no dialog at all.
# NOTE: this does NOT restrict Marc — switching models mid-conversation still works
# exactly as before. It only stops the START-UP script from creating an orphan modal.
# The trailing `claudemanager` is the INITIAL PROMPT, not a typed message. Since 9 Aug 2026
# a SessionStart hook shows a persona picker and tells the agent to STOP and wait for a human.
# An unattended watchdog respawn therefore parked at that menu forever — which is exactly how
# Marc lost his 5:10 brief on 10 Aug. Passing the persona name as a launch ARGUMENT answers the
# picker before it can block (verified live 10 Aug 22:2x: "matching CLAUDEMANAGER and loading it
# now", statusline [Fable 5]). Same lesson as model-by-flag: never type into the TUI.
# `--permission-mode auto` (added 15 Aug 2026) is the third instance of that same lesson.
# At 02:02 that morning a fresh manager was stopped by the dialog "Set up auto mode for
# your environment?" and sat frozen for 439 minutes. A mode that is already decided at
# launch cannot be asked about later. The value matches settings.json's existing
# permissions.defaultMode ("auto"), so behaviour is unchanged — it is simply no longer
# negotiable at runtime. Anything the boot needs arrives as a flag: model (4 Aug),
# persona (10 Aug), permission mode (15 Aug).
tmux new-session -d -s "$SESSION" -c "$WORKDIR" \
  "claude --name \"$TITLE\" --remote-control \"$TITLE\" --model \"$MODEL_PRIMARY\" --effort max --permission-mode auto claudemanager"
sleep 12
# Fallback (Marc, 3 Aug 2026): if Fable didn't take (usage limits), relaunch on Opus 5.
# Check the live status line "[Fable 5]", NOT the word "Fable" anywhere on screen — the
# old check matched the settings.json banner and so could never actually fail.
if ! alive || ! tmux capture-pane -t "$SESSION" -p | grep -q "\[Fable 5\]"; then
  tmux kill-session -t "$SESSION" 2>/dev/null
  tmux new-session -d -s "$SESSION" -c "$WORKDIR" \
    "claude --name \"$TITLE\" --remote-control \"$TITLE\" --model \"$MODEL_FALLBACK\" --effort max --permission-mode auto claudemanager"
  sleep 12
fi

# Designated victim, from the first second (18 Aug 2026). If the box runs out of memory
# in one breath — faster than any 5-minute check can react — the kernel shoots whatever
# it is told to shoot first. That must be the manager: its death is recoverable by design
# (marker → confession → transcript recovery), while postgres or a gunicorn worker taking
# the bullet is an outage with real users on it. The watchdog re-applies this every run,
# because an in-place update re-spawns the process (the 21:30 victim was a pid tmux never
# started).
PANE_PID=$(tmux list-panes -t "$SESSION" -F '#{pane_pid}' 2>/dev/null | head -1)
[ -n "$PANE_PID" ] && oom_protect "$PANE_PID"
rm -f "$MEMWARN" "$EMERG" "$PARKSEEN"   # a fresh session starts with a clean slate

# Wait until the TUI has no running turn.
# The old version grepped `tail -6` for "tokens" and returned instantly EVERY time —
# that single bug is what queued the slash commands that froze the manager. Two errors
# in it, both measured live 4 Aug 2026:
#   1. wrong region — the spinner renders in the CONTENT area (pane line ~16), so
#      `tail -6` never saw it;
#   2. wrong string — there is no "tokens" in the spinner in v2.1.221.
# BUSY_RE — the real discriminator, and the note on why the glyph must never be matched
# — now lives at the TOP of this file: the watchdog needs the same test before it may
# send a single key, so there is exactly one definition of "is a turn running".
wait_idle() {
  local max=${1:-180} t=0
  sleep 8   # let the turn actually start rendering before the first look
  while tmux capture-pane -t "$SESSION" -p | grep -qE "$BUSY_RE"; do
    sleep 3; t=$((t+3)); [ "$t" -ge "$max" ] && break
  done
  sleep 2
  # Never type into a session sitting on a modal — that is how messages get queued
  # and silently lost. Clear it first.
  parked && { tmux send-keys -t "$SESSION" Enter; sleep 3; }
}

# The persona already arrived as the LAUNCH ARGUMENT above, and it self-boots the moment
# it loads (Session 2 discovery #9). Typing "claudemanager" here as well — a leftover from
# before 10 Aug 2026, when the picker still had to be answered by hand — made the manager
# load its persona and run its whole boot sequence TWICE, every rotate, on Fable at max
# effort. Removed 15 Aug 2026 after Marc read a boot log and saw the duplication.
# The wait stays: the launch-arg boot turn must finish before the safety-net Boot message.
wait_idle 240   # persona load + self-boot turn can run over a minute (seen 1m13s live)
tmux send-keys -t "$SESSION" "Boot: run your boot sequence from the persona (handover check, open-check, heartbeat, schedule next wake-up), then stand by quietly."
sleep 1
tmux send-keys -t "$SESSION" Enter   # separate Enter: long text trips paste-detection, where inline Enter = newline, not submit

echo "✅ Manager starting as '$TITLE' (tmux: $SESSION, dir: $WORKDIR)."
echo "   Phone: claude.ai/code → session '$TITLE ...'"
echo "   Watch: claudemanager console · Health: claudemanager status · Stop: claudemanager stop"
